    "summary": "Somaliland's president stated that his administration is prepared to contribute up to 2,000 soldiers to a planned international stabilization force for Gaza, pending a formal request from the United States or Israel.",
    "content": "Somaliland's President Abdirahman Mohamed Abdullahi has indicated that his administration is prepared to contribute as many as 2,000 soldiers to a multinational stabilization force that is being considered for Gaza. In an interview conducted on the margins of the MEAD conference, Abdullahi said that Somaliland has not volunteered its troops but would be ready to respond if a formal request is made by the United States or Israel. \"If they ask, we can provide,\" he said. \"If they ask me, I am ready.\" The comments were first reported by The Jerusalem Post.\n\nThe proposal comes amid ongoing international discussions about how to secure and stabilize Gaza following the recent conflict. Several countries have been approached to contribute personnel to a potential mission, though the details of such a force—including its mandate, command structure, and deployment timeline—remain under negotiation. Somaliland, which declared independence from Somalia in 1991 but has not received widespread international recognition, has previously participated in peacekeeping efforts in the region, including in Sierra Leone and Liberia.\n\nPresident Abdullahi, who took office in 2017, has sought to raise Somaliland's international profile and strengthen ties with the United States and other countries. His willingness to consider deploying troops to Gaza may be seen as part of that broader diplomatic outreach. However, any such contribution would likely require approval from Somaliland's parliament and could face logistical challenges, given the territory's limited military resources and distance from Gaza.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the development raises several questions. Somaliland is often viewed by diaspora communities as a separate entity from Somalia, and its potential involvement in a Gaza force could further complicate perceptions of Somali representation on the international stage. Some diaspora members may worry that participation in a Middle East mission could entangle Somaliland in regional conflicts, while others might see it as an opportunity to demonstrate Somaliland's capacity for global engagement. The Somali federal government, which claims sovereignty over Somaliland, has not commented on the matter.\n\nIt remains unclear whether the United States or Israel will make a formal request, and no timeline has been given for a decision. The idea of an international stabilization force for Gaza is still in early stages, with multiple countries reportedly considering contributions. Somaliland's offer, if pursued, would mark a significant shift in its foreign policy, as it has traditionally focused on neighboring regions.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somaliland-ready-send-2000-troops-gaza-president-irro/",
