    "title": "East African Law Society Warns Kenya Over Harassment of Foreign Traders",
    "original_title": "Ururka Qareennada Bariga Afrika oo Kenya uga digay cabburinta ganacsatada ajaanibta",
    "slug": "east-african-law-society-warns-kenya-over-harassment-of-foreign-traders",
    "summary": "The East African Law Society (EALS) has cautioned Kenya against targeting foreign traders in ways that violate its constitution and EAC treaty obligations, citing rising fears among EAC citizens in Kenya.",
    "content": "The East African Law Society (EALS) has issued a warning to the Kenyan government regarding its treatment of foreign traders, particularly those from other East African Community (EAC) member states. The regional legal body expressed concern that recent measures targeting foreign business owners may contravene Kenya's own constitution and its commitments under the EAC treaty. EALS emphasized that an individual's nationality alone cannot be used as proof that they are residing or trading in the country illegally.\n\nThe warning comes amid growing anxiety among EAC citizens living and working in Kenya, especially those operating small-scale businesses. EALS noted that these traders have faced increasing harassment, which it says is inconsistent with the rights and protections guaranteed under regional agreements. The organization called on Kenyan authorities to ensure that any enforcement actions are carried out in full compliance with the law and without discrimination.\n\nKenya has previously taken steps to regulate foreign participation in certain sectors, often citing the need to protect local businesses and ensure tax compliance. However, such measures have at times raised concerns about their impact on regional integration and the free movement of persons and goods within the EAC. The EAC treaty provides for non-discrimination among citizens of member states, allowing them to live and work in any partner country under certain conditions.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the development is particularly significant. Many Somalis are citizens of EAC member states, including Kenya itself, and have built livelihoods as traders and entrepreneurs across the region. The warning by EALS highlights the risk of broader crackdowns that could affect their businesses and residency status. It also underscores the importance of legal safeguards against arbitrary treatment based on nationality.\n\nEALS has urged Kenya to uphold its constitutional and international obligations, reminding authorities that any action against foreign traders must be lawful and justified. The organization's intervention serves as a reminder that regional bodies are monitoring the situation and may hold governments accountable for violations. For the diaspora, this reinforces the need for vigilance and legal awareness in navigating immigration and business regulations.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/10/ururka-qareennada-bariga-afrika-oo-kenya-uga-digay-cabburinta-ganacsatada-ajaanibta/",
