    "original_title": "Muwaadin Soomaaliyeed oo magangelyo-doon ah oo lagu hayo Guantánamo Bay",
    "slug": "somali-asylum-seeker-held-at-guant-namo-bay-camp-six",
    "summary": "A 31-year-old Somali citizen, Aadam Cabdullaahi, has been detained at the U.S. military base in Guantánamo Bay, Cuba, for nearly two weeks after initially being told he would be deported to Somalia.",
    "content": "A 31-year-old Somali citizen, Aadam Cabdullaahi, is being held at Camp Six, a detention facility at the U.S. military base in Guantánamo Bay, Cuba, according to his own account. Cabdullaahi stated that he was detained by U.S. immigration officials and has been at the facility for nearly two weeks.\n\nHe said that agents from U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) initially informed him that he would be returned to Somalia. Instead, he was transferred to Camp Six, where U.S. authorities hold individuals pending further legal steps. The camp is part of the larger Guantánamo Bay detention complex, which has long been used to hold persons in U.S. custody.\n\nThe transfer of a Somali asylum seeker to Guantánamo Bay is unusual, as the facility is primarily associated with non-citizens held on terrorism-related suspicions. Cabdullaahi's case appears to stem from immigration violations rather than criminal charges, though the exact legal basis for his detention remains unclear. U.S. officials have not publicly commented on his situation.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development raises concerns about the treatment of Somali nationals in U.S. immigration custody. It also highlights the risks faced by those seeking asylum, particularly when deportation to Somalia may not be safe due to ongoing instability. Community advocates have previously criticized the use of prolonged detention and offshore facilities for immigration purposes.\n\nThis case may prompt diaspora groups to call for greater transparency and due process for Somali detainees. It also underscores the broader challenges in U.S. immigration policy, where individuals can be held for extended periods without clear resolution. The Somali government has not issued a statement on the matter.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/10/muwaadin-soomaaliyeed-oo-magangelyo-doon-ah-oo-lagu-hayo-guantanamo-bay/",
